# Globasa Constraint Grammar for POS disambiguation
#
# Algorithm:
#   Section 1: Resolve b-class (n/vblex) via prepositions, tense particles,
#              determiners, possessives, and numbers — scanning through
#              intervening t-class (adj/adv) and d;m-class (det/adv) words.
#   Section 2: SVO disambiguation + lone-b fallback, all in one section
#              iterating to fixed point so rules can chain on each other's
#              outputs (rule 88 disambiguates a sentence-initial b → makes
#              rule 91 fire on the next cohort → makes rule 94 fire on the
#              one after that).  Each rule guards itself against the other
#              conjunct of a `ji` being still ambiguous, so coordinated
#              parallel structures stay safe until Section 3 propagates.
#   Section 3: Parallel-structure propagation across coordinating CONJ —
#              once the previous conjunct's head is unambiguously a verb,
#              the next conjunct's b inherits that V tag too. The noun
#              counterpart is unnecessary: rule 88's NEGATE form already
#              defaults lone-b's to N when no verb signal reaches them.
#   Section 4: Resolve t-class (adj vs adv) and d;m-class (det vs adv)
#              based on the resolved POS of the next connected b-class word.
#   Section 5: Interjection cleanup.
#
# Globasa phrase structure:
#   Noun phrase:  (Det) + (PossAdj) + (Quantifier) + (AdvMod) + (Adj) + NOUN
#   Verb phrase:  (Tense) + (si/no) + (AdvMod) + (Adv) + (be-) + (du-) + VERB
#
# Limitation: el-fronted word orders (S-O-V, O-S-V) are rare (poetry/lyrics)
#             and may not resolve correctly under the SVO heuristic.

DELIMITERS = "<.>" "<!>" "<?>" "<…>" "<...>" ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

LIST Nom = n prn ;                  # Nominals: nouns and pronouns
LIST AnyVerb = vblex vbser vbmod ;  # Any verbal reading
LIST AnyNV = n vblex vbser ;        # Any noun or verb (for scanning)

# Barrier set: everything that is NOT an adjective or adverb.
# Scanning through adj/adv is transparent; anything else blocks.
LIST NotAdjAdv = n vblex vbser vbmod vaux prn pr vbpr det pos num
                 cnjsub cnjcoo cnjadv cm sent ij ;

# Clause boundaries reset SVO context. cnjcoo IS still here for Sections 2
# and 4 so each coord conjunct disambiguates independently first; Section 3
# then propagates the resolved head of the previous conjunct across `ji`.
LIST Clause = cnjsub cnjcoo cnjadv cm sent ;

# Combined sets for compound barriers.
SET AnyVerbOrClause = AnyVerb OR Clause ;

# Things that interrupt the t→b connection (for Section 5).
SET TConnect = (pr) OR (vbpr) OR Clause ;


# ===========================================================================
# SECTION 1: Resolve b-class via prepositions, particles, determiners, etc.
# ===========================================================================
# A preposition/det/poss/num projects forward through t/d;m words to the
# next b-class word, resolving it as NOUN.  A tense particle (vbpr) projects
# forward similarly, resolving the next b as VERB.
#
# CBARRIER is used instead of BARRIER so that d;m words (det+adv) are
# transparent: CBARRIER only blocks when ALL readings match the set.

SECTION

# hay is always a verb (special: allows subject after verb).
SELECT (vblex) IF (0 ("hay")) ;

# b.lin followed by t-class (adj/adv) → copula (vbser)
SELECT (vbser) IF (1* (adj) BARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;

# b after preposition → noun
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* (pr) CBARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;

# b after tense particle → verb
REMOVE (n) IF (-1* (vbpr) CBARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;

# b after determiner → noun
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* (det) CBARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;

# b after possessive adjective → noun
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* (pos) CBARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;

# b after number → noun
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* (num) CBARRIER NotAdjAdv) ;


# ===========================================================================
# SECTION 2: SVO disambiguation + lone-b fallback
# ===========================================================================
# All three SVO rules live in one section so CG3's iteration can chain
# their effects: rule 88 disambiguates a sentence-initial / cross-cnjcoo
# lone-b → rule 91 then fires on the cohort that depends on it → rule 94
# fires next. Each rule guards against still-ambiguous neighbours so a
# parallel verb on the other side of `ji` (e.g. `Patre mwa ji glu matre`)
# isn't prematurely turned into a noun.

SECTION

# Rule 88: Lone-b fallback. Any `b` with neither a Nom-or-V to its left
# within the (cnjcoo-bounded) clause becomes a noun.
#
# NEGATE (not NOT) is essential so the test passes when the leftward scan
# is *blocked by a BARRIER* without finding the target. CG3 treats NOT as
# stricter ("no target AND scan not aborted") and so silently failed at
# every clause split — the original rlx's lone-b rule basically only ever
# fired at sentence start.
#
# The third condition (NEGATE -1 (cnjcoo) LINK -1 (vblex)) protects
# parallel-verb constructs: when the cohort is preceded by `ji` and the
# previous conjunct still has a vblex reading (whether unambig or not
# yet disambiguated), defer to Section 3's parallel-verb SELECT rather
# than defaulting this b to a noun.
REMOVE (vblex) IF (NEGATE -1* Nom BARRIER Clause)
                  (NEGATE -1* (vblex) BARRIER Clause)
                  (NEGATE -1 (cnjcoo) LINK -1 (vblex)) ;

# Rule 91: b after a noun/pronoun with no verb between → verb.
#
# The `NEGATE -1* (vblex) BARRIER AnyVerbOrClause` guard prevents this
# rule from misfiring when the closest left cohort is itself a still-
# ambiguous `b` (whose `n` reading would otherwise look like a clause
# subject). Without the guard, `matre` in `Patre mwa ji glu matre` treats
# the unresolved `glu` as a noun-subject and gets pruned to vblex before
# Section 3 can resolve `glu` as a parallel verb.
REMOVE (n) IF (-1* Nom BARRIER AnyVerbOrClause)
              (NEGATE -1* (vblex) BARRIER AnyVerbOrClause) ;

# Rule 94: b after a verb → object (noun).
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* AnyVerb BARRIER Clause) ;


# ===========================================================================
# SECTION 3: Parallel-structure propagation across coordinating CONJ
# ===========================================================================
# Once Section 2 has disambiguated each conjunct in isolation, propagate
# the head's POS across `ji`/`or`/`mas`/... so that
#   Patre mwa ji glu matre → mwa V (S2) → glu V (S3) → then S3's re-run
#   of the verb-left rule makes matre N.
# Noun coord (`Vyata ji imanu sen muhim`) is already handled by rule 88's
# lone-b fallback in Section 2.

SECTION

# Parallel-verb: SELECT vblex on a b whose immediate left is `cnjcoo` and
# whose neighbour through the coordinator is an unambiguous verb (no n
# reading remaining). The `NEGATE -2 (n)` guard ensures the previous
# conjunct has fully resolved before this fires.
SELECT (vblex) IF (-1 (cnjcoo)) (-2 (vblex)) (NEGATE -2 (n)) ;

# Once SELECT has forced the right conjunct to vblex, re-apply the
# verb-left rule so a trailing object resolves too (e.g. `matre` in
# `Patre mwa ji glu matre`).
REMOVE (vblex) IF (-1* AnyVerb BARRIER Clause) ;


# ===========================================================================
# SECTION 3: Resolve t-class (adj/adv) and d;m-class (det/adv)
# ===========================================================================
# Look right for the next b-class word not interrupted by a preposition,
# tense particle, or clause boundary.
#   - If that b is a noun → t is ADJ, d;m is DET.
#   - If that b is a verb → t is ADV, d;m is ADV.
#   - If punctuation/clause boundary intervenes → rules 1-3 fail, fall to default.
# Special: t after copula (vbser) with no following b in clause → ADJ.
# Default: ADV.

SECTION

# t connected to following NOUN-not-verb → adjective.
# The barrier includes AnyVerb so a t standing before a verb (e.g.
# `Le velosi yam pingo`) doesn't look past the verb to the object noun
# and mis-tag itself as ADJ. The nearest b's category wins.
SELECT (adj) IF (1* (n) BARRIER TConnect OR AnyVerb) ;

# d;m connected to following NOUN-not-verb → determiner
SELECT (det) IF (1* (n) BARRIER TConnect OR AnyVerb) ;

# t or d;m connected to following VERB-not-noun → adverb
SELECT (adv) IF (1* AnyVerb BARRIER TConnect OR (n) OR (prn)) ;

# t after copula with no following noun/verb in clause → adjective (predicate).
# NEGATE (not NOT) so the test passes when the rightward scan is blocked
# by a Clause barrier without reaching an NV target — same CG3 quirk that
# previously kept rule 88's lone-b fallback silent at every clause split.
SELECT (adj) IF (-1* (vbser) BARRIER NotAdjAdv) (NEGATE 1* AnyNV BARRIER Clause) ;

# Default: adverb
SELECT (adv) ;


# ===========================================================================
# SECTION 4: Interjection cleanup
# ===========================================================================
# Remove interjection readings when adjacent to content words.

SECTION

REMOVE (ij) IF (-1 Nom) ;
REMOVE (ij) IF (1 Nom) ;
REMOVE (ij) IF (-1 (vblex)) ;
REMOVE (ij) IF (1 (vblex)) ;
REMOVE (ij) IF (1 (adv)) ;

# Resources:
# https://edu.visl.dk/cg3/chunked/
# https://wiki.apertium.org/wiki/Constraint_Grammar
# https://wiki.apertium.org/wiki/Apertium_and_Constraint_Grammar
